{
  "id": 238522950,
  "iid": 11498,
  "project_id": 7898047,
  "title": "MySQL/MariaDB: Dissect CLIENT_SSL_VERIFY_SERVER_CERT bit",
  "description": "Many MySQL and MariaDB clients *do* send the `CLIENT_SSL_VERIFY_SERVER_CERT`\nbit on the wire, in their pre-TLS-handshake client packet. I have personally\nverified this against the following clients:\n\n- MySQL 8.0.33 (`mysql` CLI)\n- MariaDB 10.2.44 (`mysql` CLI)\n- MariaDB 11.2.0 (`mariadb`/`mysql` CLI)\n\nThis is a very dangerous thing to put on the wire in plaintext: putting\n`CLIENT_SSL_VERIFY_SERVER_CERT=0` on the wire basically says \"feel free\nto undetectably MITM this TLS connection.\"\n\nThe MySQL/MariaDB client vulnerability\nhttps://jira.mariadb.org/browse/CONC-656 can be easily detected with this\ndissector patch in place, and indeed *was* discovered due to my\nexperimentation here.\n\nAll new code of the whole pull request, including one or several files\nthat are either new files or modified ones, are contributed under the\nBSD-new license. I am contributing on behalf of my employer Amazon Web\nServices, Inc.",
  "state": "merged",
